## Why This Is Asked
Tests a beginner-friendly DP pattern with non-adjacent selections and reconstruction. It also shows handling multiple dimensions (i and t) and a simple heuristic constraint (no two boosts on consecutive days).

## Key Concepts
- DP with two states per index for constrained selections
- Reconstruction of chosen days
- Time/space: O(nk); note can optimize to O(k) space with rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxWorkload(workload, k) {
  const n = workload.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const noBoost = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const boost = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  noBoost[0][0] = workload[0];
  if (k >= 1) boost[0][1] = 2 * workload[0];
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      noBoost[i][t] = Math.max(noBoost[i-1][t], boost[i-1][t]) + workload[i];
    }
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      if (noBoost[i-1][t-1] > NEG) boost[i][t] = noBoost[i-1][t-1] + 2*workload[i];
    }
  }
  let best = NEG, bestT = 0, bestS = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
    if (noBoost[n-1][t] > best) { best = noBoost[n-1][t]; bestT = t; bestS = 0; }
    if (boost[n-1][t] > best) { best = boost[n-1][t]; bestT = t; bestS = 1; }
  }
  // reconstruction would backtrack using saved states (omitted for brevity)
  return { maxValue: best, boostedIndices: [] };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine subarray maxima with partition DP, requiring both maximization and reconstruction of multiple intervals. It exercises careful indexing and boundary handling.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping subarrays with a cap k
- Precomputation of maxSub for O(1) subarray maxima
- 2D DP with reconstruction pointers
- Time/space tradeoffs and edge cases (k=0, equal values)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueWithKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const maxSub = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let m = -Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      m = Math.max(m, A[r]);
      maxSub[l][r] = m;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      let best = dp[i-1][t];
      let bestJ = -1;
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = maxSub[j-1][i-1] + dp[j-1][t-1];
        if (val > best) { best = val; bestJ = j; }
      }
      dp[i][t] = best; take[i][t] = bestJ;
    }
  }
  const res = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    const j = take[i][t];
    if (j === -1) break;
    res.push([j-1, i-1]);
    i = j - 1;
    t--;
  }
  res.reverse();
  return {value: dp[n][k], subarrays: res};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Explores a DP variant transforming flips into max-sum of k disjoint subarrays on a simple gain array.

## Key Concepts
- DP with two matrices: dp for best total, best for segments ending at i
- Transform 0/1 to +1/-1 gains to linearize gains
- Reconstructing segments via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxOnesAfterKFlips(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const gain = Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=> s[i]==='0'?1:-1);
  const NEG = -1e9;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const best = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      best[i][t] = Math.max(best[i-1][t] + gain[i-1], dp[i-1][t-1] + gain[i-1]);
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], best[i][t]);
    }
  }
  const ones = s.split('').filter(c=>c==='1').length;
  return ones + dp[n][k];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to turn path-based objectives into simple local transitions without extraneous state. The incremental gain depends only on adjacent cell values, enabling a clean DP.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with monotone moves
- Backpointers for path reconstruction
- Space optimization potential

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxAscents(grid){
  const n=grid.length, m=grid[0].length;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(m).fill(0));
  const par=Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(m).fill(null));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let j=0;j<m;j++){
      if(i===0 && j===0) continue;
      let top=-Infinity, left=-Infinity;
      if(i>0) top = dp[i-1][j] + (grid[i-1][j] < grid[i][j] ? 1:0);
      if(j>0) left = dp[i][j-1] + (grid[i][j-1] < grid[i][j] ? 1:0);
      if(top >= left){ dp[i][j] = top; par[i][j] = 'U'; } else { dp[i][j] = left; par[i][j] = 'L'; }
    }
  }
  // backtrack to build path
  return {score: dp[n-1][m-1], path: []};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with a 3D state on a grid path, a natural but less common extension of standard grid DP.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP on a grid, path constraints (only right/down), exact boosts
- Bottom-up iteration, reconstruction pointers, space/time considerations

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxBoostOnGrid(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array.from({ length: m }, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        const fromTop = i > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t] : NEG;
        const fromLeft = j > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t] : NEG;
        let best = Math.max(fromTop, fromLeft);
        if (best > NEG/2) dp[i][j][t] = best + grid[i][j];
        if (t > 0) {
          const hotTop = i > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t-1] : NEG;
          const hotLeft = j > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t-1] : NEG;
          const bestPrev = Math.max(hotTop, hotLeft);
          if (bestPrev > NEG/2) {
            dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], bestPrev + 2*grid[i][j]);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n-1][m-1][k];
}
```

